<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="UDP-β-L-arabinose biosynthesis I (from UDP-α-D-xylose)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-63" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; L-Arabinose, the 4-epimer of D-xylose, is a monosaccharide found primarily in plants. It is mainly present in cell wall polymers such as pectic (rhamnogalacturonan I, rhamnogalacturonan II) and hemicellulosic (xylan, xyloglucan) components, as well as in the glycosyl-moieties of proteins such as arabinogalactan proteins and extensions |CITS:[10517829],[10640355]|. Although little is known of the incorporation of arabinose into these polymers or the contribution of the arabinosyl-substituents to the functions of the wall polymers, it has been demonstrated that arabinose-containing polymers are derived from UDP-L-arabinose, the activated sugar used by arabinosyltransferases |CITS:[FEINGOLD80]|. &lt;b&gt;This Pathway in Plants:&lt;/b&gt; The formation of UDP-L-arabinose occurs via two routes: a) the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis route (this pathway) involves the epimerisation of UDP-D-xylose by a UDP-D-xylose 4-epimerase, and b) a salvage pathway leading to the formation of UDP-L-arabinose from free arabinose (|FRAME:PWY-82|). &lt;b&gt;Key Notes on the Enzymes:&lt;/b&gt; Few UDP-D-xylose 4-epimerases have been described in the literature although this enzymatic activity has been described for several plants |CITS:[192218],[192219],[13821949],[5483919]|. One has been purified from wheat germ extract. When the enzymatic activity of this enzyme was tested, the reversible conversion of UDP-L-arabinose (UDP-L-Ara) to UDP-D-xylose (UDP-D-Xyl) was achieved with an equilibrium constant of 1.25 |CITS:[5483919]|. More recently, the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana MUR4&lt;/i&gt; mutant was described which was shown to be affected in its ability to epimerize UDP-D-Xyl to UDP-L-Ara |CITS:[10517829],[12566589]|. Although the MUR4 protein was expressed in transgenic yeast cells and shown to possess UDP-D-xylose 4-epimerase activity, the corresponding enzyme has not yet been fully purified. &lt;b&gt;Important Note:&lt;/b&gt; In cell wall the majority of arabinose is in a furanose, not pyranose, configuration. &lt;b&gt;This Pathway in Bacteria&lt;/b&gt;: There is also evidence for this pathway in plant-associated bacteria belonging to the family |FRAME: TAX-82115|. Rhizobia are soil bacteria that fix nitrogen in the root nodules of some leguminous plants. In |FRAME: TAX-266834| an operon was identified that contains genes &lt;i&gt;uxe&lt;/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;uxs1&lt;/i&gt; encoding UDP-xylose 4-epimerase and UDP-xylose synthase 1, respectively. Data from real-time &lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;H-NMR spectroscopy demonstrated the conversion of |FRAME: UDP-GLUCURONATE| to |FRAME: UDP-D-XYLOSE|, and |FRAME: UDP-D-XYLOSE| to |FRAME: CPD-12513|. These UDP-sugars are then used for the biosynthesis of xylose-containing and arabinose-containing cell surface glycans. Their pathways and the roles that they play in nodulation are only beginning to be elucidated |CITS: [20847005]|. Two orthologs of each gene encoding active enzymes were also identified in other rhizobial species including |FRAME: TAX-216596| (loci R10559 and R10078 for &lt;i&gt;uxe&lt;/i&gt;, R10560 and R10077 for &lt;i&gt;uxs&lt;/i&gt;). These observations were cited in |CITS: [20847005]| as unpublished data.</Description>
